WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:12.527
FAYE MOSS: Hello, everyone! Welcome back to Superstars in History —the fashionable talk show with famous people from the past. I'm your host,

2
00:00:12.528 --> 00:00:22.945
Faye Moss. Today's guest played a big part in changing women's lives by changing women's clothes and creating some looks that are still popular

3
00:00:22.946 --> 00:00:43.166
today. Straight from history, here is fashion legend and businesswoman Gabrielle "Coco" Chanel!

4
00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:48.395
COCO CHANEL: Thank you, thank

5
00:00:48.396 --> 00:00:52.447
you. Merci . FAYE MOSS: Welcome to the show, Gabrielle—or should

6
00:00:52.448 --> 00:00:54.893
I call you Coco?

7
00:00:54.894 --> 00:01:00.454
COCO CHANEL: Call me Coco, Faye. FAYE MOSS: How did you get the name

8
00:01:00.455 --> 00:01:08.150
Coco? Is that what your parents called you? COCO CHANEL: No. My parents . . . well, I barely knew my father, and

9
00:01:08.151 --> 00:01:13.155
my mother died when I was 11, in 1895. I was put in a convent in central France where

10
00:01:13.156 --> 00:01:18.057
nuns took care of girls with no families. I learned how to sew from the nuns

11
00:01:18.058 --> 00:01:23.959
there, which became quite a useful skill! As for the name Coco, after I turned 18, I was too

12
00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.290
old to stay at the convent. I moved to a different town and worked

13
00:01:27.291 --> 00:01:32.276
as a seamstress by day. However, I really wanted to be a performer, so I sang

14
00:01:32.277 --> 00:01:37.965
at a café at night. I became popular by singing the song

15
00:01:37.966 --> 00:01:41.030
"Who Has Seen Coco?" Soon people were calling

16
00:01:41.031 --> 00:01:45.194
me by that name! FAYE MOSS: Wow, you were a singer before

17
00:01:45.195 --> 00:01:51.432
you were a fashion icon? You must have been really talented. Why

18
00:01:51.433 --> 00:01:55.308
don't you sing for us? COCO CHANEL: No, no.

19
00:01:55.309 --> 00:02:02.942
I actually didn't have much talent! By my early twenties, I realized that my singing voice wasn't good enough for

20
00:02:02.943 --> 00:02:06.353
me to become a professional. FAYE MOSS: How did you

21
00:02:06.354 --> 00:02:09.690
get started in the world of fashion? COCO CHANEL: I was poor and

22
00:02:09.691 --> 00:02:13.376
needed a way to make a better living. Luckily, I'd

23
00:02:13.377 --> 00:02:18.303
made the acquaintance of some rich society people. Since I'd already been making hats for fun, I

24
00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:24.639
decided to turn it into a business. I was able to set up my own shop in 1910, with some

25
00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.290
financial help from my wealthy friends. My hat designs quickly

26
00:02:29.291 --> 00:02:32.484
became popular with some famous actresses.

27
00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:40.377
FAYE MOSS: What made them so popular? COCO CHANEL: They were simple! Hats at the time were complex,

28
00:02:40.378 --> 00:02:43.584
heavy creations, with flowers and bows

29
00:02:43.585 --> 00:02:48.118
and frill. These ornate designs weighed women down. All of women's fashion

30
00:02:48.119 --> 00:02:53.782
in the 1900s and 1910s was like this. Women wore tight corsets with bone or steel that

31
00:02:53.783 --> 00:02:58.960
were uncomfortable and hard to move in. Dresses were tight and had

32
00:02:58.961 --> 00:03:03.467
many layers—the horror of it all! In fact, one evening I wore a dress that was so tight that

33
00:03:03.468 --> 00:03:08.372
I had to loosen it. But then I couldn't fasten it again, and I had

34
00:03:08.373 --> 00:03:12.910
nothing to cover up with! FAYE MOSS: It sounds

35
00:03:12.911 --> 00:03:21.799
like women's fashion was really inconvenient. COCO CHANEL: Oui , yes! That's why, when I opened my first clothing boutique in 1913, I wanted

36
00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:26.767
to make clothes that were comfortable for women. I designed

37
00:03:26.768 --> 00:03:31.846
simple skirts, jackets, and blouses using light fabrics. Women could easily move, enjoy sports,

38
00:03:31.847 --> 00:03:36.078
and even ride horses in these clothes. Back then, horseback

39
00:03:36.079 --> 00:03:39.926
riding was a favorite activity of mine. Best of all, women didn't have to

40
00:03:39.927 --> 00:03:46.561
wear corsets while wearing the clothing. Two years later I opened a second store. And during World

41
00:03:46.562 --> 00:03:53.243
War I, my sales increased. FAYE MOSS: That's surprising. Why did your clothes continue

42
00:03:53.244 --> 00:03:57.325
to be popular even during wartime?

43
00:03:57.326 --> 00:04:02.990
COCO CHANEL: Fashion, is place and time, Faye. My designs were right for

44
00:04:02.991 --> 00:04:09.731
the time—subdued and simple, not fancy and fussy. I admit, I was inspired by military uniforms too, so my

45
00:04:09.732 --> 00:04:17.328
designs were in plain colors and functional. In a time as dreadful as war, when young men were dying daily, no one wanted to

46
00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:19.828
dress in the showy prewar style. After

47
00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:25.240
the war, fashion changed again. Women, including me, began bobbing their

48
00:04:25.241 --> 00:04:27.947
hair and wearing shorter skirts. FAYE MOSS:

49
00:04:27.948 --> 00:04:36.130
That was in the 1920s, right? Around that time, you began creating a look that's still popular today,

50
00:04:36.131 --> 00:04:46.117
which many people call a timeless— COCO CHANEL: You mean this suit? Men, of course, had worn suits for a long time, and even I wore

51
00:04:46.118 --> 00:04:49.581
men's suits occasionally—they were very comfortable. "Why didn't women

52
00:04:49.582 --> 00:04:54.966
have an equivalent garment?" I wondered. So, I decided to make one—a

53
00:04:54.967 --> 00:05:03.723
comfortable matching jacket and skirt. I chose to use wool or tweed since I visited England and Scotland often, and I'd noticed that men's sporting suits were often

54
00:05:03.724 --> 00:05:09.477
made with those materials. To be sure that women felt comfortable in my suits, I didn't just take their measurements before

55
00:05:09.478 --> 00:05:18.352
making the clothing. I watched my clients move as they would in their everyday lives . . . sitting down, opening doors, getting into cars, walking

56
00:05:18.353 --> 00:05:24.702
up stairs. FAYE MOSS: It sounds like you thought of everything! No wonder your suit became

57
00:05:24.703 --> 00:05:27.512
such a classic. COCO CHANEL:

58
00:05:27.513 --> 00:05:33.754
Yes, it was very popular. I closed my shops during World War II, and I didn't return to designing clothing

59
00:05:33.755 --> 00:05:46.240
for quite some time. I was busy working on other things. Finally, in 1954, I had my first fashion show

60
00:05:46.241 --> 00:05:56.914
in 15 years. But by then many new male designers were working in the industry, and the latest designs for women featured full, heavy skirts and

61
00:05:56.915 --> 00:06:00.631
tight waists. I did not like this "New Look," as it was

62
00:06:00.632 --> 00:06:06.110
called. I had made clothes freer for women, and the postwar styles were restrictive

63
00:06:06.111 --> 00:06:11.888
again. My new collection featured the simple, elegant suits and dresses I had always made.

64
00:06:11.889 --> 00:06:21.460
The French press wasn't very kind about my show—they said that the new Chanel line was outdated, unchanged since before the war. But

65
00:06:21.461 --> 00:06:26.672
American fashion editors promoted my designs in their magazines. FAYE MOSS:

66
00:06:26.673 --> 00:06:32.382
Your designs definitely changed fashion, and many are still popular today.

67
00:06:32.383 --> 00:06:36.937
I wonder what women would be wearing today without your influence. Thank

68
00:06:36.938 --> 00:06:43.510
you very much, Coco, for being on the show. We'll let you get back to history now. COCO CHANEL:

69
00:06:43.511 --> 00:06:49.463
Au revoir ,

70
00:06:49.464 --> 00:06:55.778
Faye! FAYE MOSS: Thank you, everyone, for joining my interview with the legendary fashion

71
00:06:55.779 --> 00:07:04.378
designer Coco Chanel. After her comeback in 1954, Coco Chanel continued to work, cutting fabric for her

72
00:07:04.379 --> 00:07:10.024
designs herself. In 1971 she died in Paris at the age of

73
00:07:10.025 --> 00:07:17.926
87. The company with her name is still famous, and her original Paris boutique and workshop are still in operation.

74
00:07:17.927 --> 00:07:24.554
I hope you enjoyed the show, and I'll see you next week with another surprise guest from the past. You've

75
00:07:24.569 --> 00:07:36.287
been watching Faye Moss on Superstars in History !

